

UNIVERSAL GOVERNMENT TRANSFORMATION ARCHITECTURE (UGTA)

A General Framework for AI-Native Public Administration and Digital State Infrastructure

ouadi Maakoul 

Claude, chatGpt.

ABSTRACT

Public administrations worldwide operate on a historically accumulated substrate of paper documents, fragmented databases, dispersed legal rules, and human-mediated workflows. Digital transformation attempts have been predominantly sectoral and architecturally incoherent, producing isolated digital islands rather than sovereign, intelligent state infrastructure. This dissertation introduces the Universal Government Transformation Architecture (UGTA), a comprehensive theoretical and engineering framework for the systematic transformation of any public administration into an AI-native, citizen-centric, auditable, and democratically governable digital organization.

UGTA rests on a foundational formal claim: any public administration can be fully represented as a four-element structure A = (D, R, W, Dec), where D denotes the documentary corpus, R the normative ruleset, W the workflow graph, and Dec the decision space. This Administrative Tetrad is the ontological primitive from which all five architectural layers of UGTA are derived. The five layers — Digital Memory Infrastructure (DMI), Government Knowledge Graph (GKG), AI Administrative Agent Network (AAAN), Formal Verification and Audit Layer (FVAL), and Democratic Governance Shell (DGS) — form a strictly hierarchical dependency chain with formally specified inter-layer contracts.

The dissertation makes five principal scientific contributions: (1) the Administrative Tetrad as a formal model of government, with proofs of completeness and decidability bounds; (2) a Migration Complexity Theory quantifying the paper-to-digital transformation cost function for heterogeneous administrative corpora; (3) the UGTA layered architecture as a universal specification comparable in scope and intent to the Internet Protocol suite; (4) a Formal Verification and Audit Framework for AI administrative decisions grounded in provenance logic, causal traceability, and legal conformance certificates; and (5) a Cognitive Yield Index (CYI) as a quantitative productivity measure for AI-augmented public administration. The framework is evaluated against four empirical cases — Estonia, Singapore, India's UIDAI-India Stack, and Morocco — and a phased global implementation roadmap is provided spanning 2025–2040.

Keywords: AI-native government, public administration formalization, knowledge graph, algorithmic governance, administrative auditability, digital state infrastructure, government transformation theory.

