CHAPTER 6. THE FORMAL VERIFICATION AND AUDIT LAYER (FVAL)

6.1 The Verification Problem in AI Governance

The deepest challenge in AI-augmented public administration is not technical capability but institutional legitimacy. AI systems are demonstrably capable of making accurate determinations in high-volume administrative domains. The question is whether those determinations can be made in a manner that is legally defensible, democratically accountable, and contestable by affected citizens — requirements that go far beyond accuracy.

Greater complexity in the discretionary decision that an AI system must adopt corresponds to lesser technical explainability of the algorithm's functioning [Frontiers](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/political-science/articles/10.3389/fpos.2025.1605729/full) . This creates a structural tension: the AI systems most capable of handling complex decisions are often the least interpretable, while the legal obligations of administrative transparency require that every decision be explicable in terms that are meaningful to both the affected citizen and a reviewing tribunal. The FVAL resolves this tension by introducing a formal layer that is positioned between the AI agent's output and the final administrative decision, and that generates legal conformance certificates whose production is itself fully auditable.

6.2 The Provenance Logic Framework

Definition 6.1 (Provenance Logic). Let PL be a formal logic for reasoning about the provenance of administrative decisions. A PL sentence is of the form:

Decision δ was produced by agent α at time t from context ctx, where ctx consists of facts {f₁, ..., fₙ} each drawn from authoritative GKG nodes {v₁, ..., vₙ} certified at times {t₁, ..., tₙ}, and the legal basis legal_basis ⊆ R was queried from the Normative Sublayer at time t, and the conditions of each rule in legal_basis were evaluated to be true given {f₁, ..., fₙ}, and the outcome is among the set of legally permissible outcomes given the satisfied obligations.

A PL sentence constitutes a complete provenance record for the decision δ.

The Provenance Logic is designed to satisfy two properties simultaneously: (a) machine-verifiability — the truth of a PL sentence can be checked algorithmically by the FVAL; and (b) legal readability — a PL sentence can be automatically translated into a natural-language statement of reasons that satisfies the legal obligation to motivate administrative decisions.

6.3 The Three Verification Checks

The FVAL performs three verification checks on every (δ_draft, context_record) submission from Layer 3.

Check V1: Legal Conformance. The FVAL queries the Normative Sublayer to verify that: (a) every rule in legal_basis was in force at the time of the decision; (b) the condition of every rule in legal_basis is satisfied by the facts in context_record; (c) no exception rule of any rule in legal_basis is activated by the facts in context_record; and (d) the outcome of δ_draft is among the deontic obligations generated by the satisfied rules.

Formally, this is a model-checking problem: check whether context_record ⊨ ∧_{rⱼ ∈ legal_basis} (condition(rⱼ) → ¬exception_active(rⱼ) → outcome_permissible(rⱼ)). By Proposition 3.3, this check is decidable in O(|legal_basis| · |R|).

Check V2: Procedural Correctness. The FVAL verifies that the workflow path leading to δ_draft is a valid execution path in the Workflow Graph W: that is, that every transition from one workflow node to the next was enabled by a satisfied guard condition, that no required workflow steps were skipped, and that the decision was generated at a node of type "decision" in W.

This is a path-validation problem on the DAG W, solvable in O(|N| + |E|) by Proposition 3.2.

Check V3: Normative Completeness. The FVAL verifies that the legal_basis of δ_draft is complete — that is, that no rule rₖ ∈ R that is relevant to the case (whose condition is satisfied by context_record) has been omitted from legal_basis. Completeness checking prevents the error of selective rule invocation, where an AI agent implicitly ignores rules whose application would alter the outcome.

Completeness checking is the most computationally demanding of the three checks, requiring evaluation of the full ruleset R against context_record. For large R, this is addressed through precomputation of a Relevance Index that partitions R by administrative domain and citizen profile type, reducing the effective search space for any given case.

6.4 The Legal Conformance Certificate

Definition 6.2 (Legal Conformance Certificate). A Legal Conformance Certificate (LCC) for a decision δ is a signed document LCC = (case_id, decision_hash, verification_time, V1_result, V2_result, V3_result, provenance_record, technical_signature, legal_summary), where:

case_id uniquely identifies the administrative case; decision_hash is a cryptographic hash of δ_draft ensuring integrity; verification_time is the timestamp of FVAL processing; V1_result, V2_result, V3_result are the binary outcomes of the three verification checks along with detailed sub-results; provenance_record is the full PL sentence establishing the decision's provenance chain; technical_signature is the digital signature of the FVAL system; and legal_summary is the automatically generated natural-language statement of reasons, legally conformant with the applicable administrative procedure law of the jurisdiction.

The LCC is the instrument through which AI administrative decisions acquire legal validity in the UGTA framework. No decision without an LCC may be finalized. The LCC itself is added to the documentary corpus D as a type "Verification Certificate" document, ensuring that the audit trail is self-contained within the UGTA system.

6.5 The Immutable Audit Ledger

The FVAL maintains an Immutable Audit Ledger (IAL) recording every verification event — successes, failures, and the full context of each. The IAL is implemented as an append-only, cryptographically chained log (analogous in structure to a blockchain but maintained by the government's own infrastructure). Properties:

Completeness: Every decision processed by the FVAL, whether certified or refused, generates an IAL entry.

Integrity: Each IAL entry contains the cryptographic hash of the preceding entry, making retrospective modification detectable.

Accessibility: The IAL is accessible to: the Layer 5 Human Governance Shell (full access); parliamentary oversight bodies (full access); the affected citizen (access to their own case entries); and the public (access to anonymized aggregate statistics).

Persistence: IAL entries are retained for a period not less than the statute of limitations applicable to administrative disputes in the jurisdiction.

6.6 Handling AI Decision Refusals

When the FVAL issues a Refusal with Diagnosis (instead of an LCC), the diagnosis document specifies: which verification check failed (V1, V2, or V3); which specific rule, workflow constraint, or normative gap caused the failure; and a structured set of options for resolution.

The resolution options may include: referral to a human civil servant for discretionary determination when the AI system lacks legal authority to decide; request for additional information from the citizen when context is insufficient; escalation to a higher-level AI agent when the case exceeds the domain agent's competence; or logging of a normative gap — a case for which the existing ruleset provides no applicable rule — triggering a regulatory review process.

Normative gaps are of particular scientific interest. They represent cases in which the law is genuinely underdetermined with respect to the facts of the case. Systematic logging of normative gaps by the UGTA system produces a continuously updated empirical map of legislative underspecification — information of immense value to legislative bodies seeking to improve the clarity and completeness of their administrative law.

