CHAPTER 7. THE COGNITIVE YIELD INDEX

7.1 Measuring the Productivity of AI-Augmented Administration

The transformation of public administration through UGTA represents an investment of extraordinary scale: political capital, financial resources, institutional energy, and time. To justify this investment, there must be a means of measuring its returns that is theoretically grounded, empirically tractable, and cross-nationally comparable. Existing administrative productivity metrics — cost per service, processing time, citizen satisfaction surveys — are insufficient: they measure individual services in isolation and do not capture the systemic intelligence gains that AI-native architecture produces.

The Cognitive Yield Index (CYI) is introduced as a comprehensive productivity measure for AI-augmented public administration, capturing both efficiency gains and quality improvements at the system level.

7.2 Formal Definition of CYI

Definition 7.1 (Cognitive Yield Index). The Cognitive Yield Index for an administration A at time t is:

CYI(A, t) = Σ_{s ∈ Services} [volume(s, t) · quality(s, t) · speed_ratio(s, t) · coverage(s, t)] / Cost(A, t)

where:

volume(s, t) is the number of administrative service transactions of type s processed in period t.

quality(s, t) ∈ [0, 1] is a composite quality measure for service s, defined as the weighted average of: accuracy (fraction of decisions that correctly apply the law, estimated by random expert audit sample); conformance (fraction of decisions that receive LCC certification on first submission); reversibility rate (fraction of decisions overturned on appeal, with lower rates indicating higher initial quality); and citizen experience (measured by structured post-interaction surveys).

speed_ratio(s, t) = benchmark_processing_time(s) / actual_processing_time(s, t), where benchmark is the OECD best-practice processing time for service s. A speed_ratio > 1 indicates performance better than best practice.

coverage(s, t) ∈ [0, 1] is the fraction of eligible citizens who actually access service s, measuring administrative reach (a critical equity dimension that pure efficiency metrics miss).

Cost(A, t) is the total operational cost of administration A in period t, normalized per capita.

7.3 Decomposition and Interpretation

The CYI admits a natural decomposition into four component indices:

CYI_E (Efficiency Component) = (volume · speed_ratio) / Cost, capturing throughput per unit cost — the classic efficiency metric.

CYI_Q (Quality Component) = (volume · quality) / Cost, capturing the quality of output produced per unit investment.

CYI_C (Coverage Component) = (volume · coverage) / Cost, capturing equity — the extent to which administrative services reach all eligible beneficiaries, including marginalized populations.

CYI_I (Integration Component) measures the fraction of services that invoke cross-agency GKG queries (exploiting the Knowledge Graph's integrative capacity) weighted by the complexity reduction achieved. Services requiring five inter-agency queries that are handled within a single AI-mediated transaction score higher than services that continue to require the citizen to collect documents from multiple agencies.

7.4 Empirical Calibration Points

Drawing on available empirical data:

Estonia's X-Road achieves a speed_ratio in excess of 20 for most standard civil services compared to EU average processing times. The coverage of Estonian e-services is effectively 1.0 for the resident population. These figures translate to a CYI substantially above any paper-based comparator.

Singapore's MyInfo system, which enables citizens to pre-fill government forms with their own GKG-equivalent data, reduces average application processing time for housing grants by approximately 80 percent and doubles coverage among previously underserved populations (Singapore GovTech Annual Report, 2024). CYI_C and CYI_E both increase substantially.

India's Aadhaar-integrated direct benefit transfer system increased coverage of social benefit recipients from approximately 40 percent to over 90 percent for certain programs, while reducing leakage (fraudulent or mistaken payments) by an estimated 40–50 percent. This produces spectacular CYI_C and CYI_Q gains.

7.5 CYI as a Policy Instrument

The CYI serves three policy functions in the UGTA framework.

First, it provides a pre-migration baseline measurement, establishing the current productivity of administration A before transformation. This baseline enables ex-post measurement of transformation impact.

Second, it enables cross-national benchmarking. By normalizing all components per capita and against domain-specific OECD benchmarks, CYI produces a score that is comparable across countries regardless of administrative size, legal tradition, or income level.

Third, it enables resource allocation within the migration. By computing CYI contribution per administrative service, UGTA migration planners can prioritize the digitization of services that will produce the greatest CYI gains per unit migration cost — a rational basis for sequencing that goes beyond the political logics that typically govern e-government priorities.

