APPENDIX A: FORMAL PROOF OF THEOREM 5.1 (MIGRATION TRACTABILITY)

We prove that conditions C1 through C4 are both necessary and sufficient for UGTA migration tractability.

Necessity. Suppose a migration succeeds within a politically and fiscally feasible horizon H. Then: (C1) must hold because without a central authority with cross-agency power, individual agencies can obstruct migration indefinitely, making H unbounded. (C2) must hold because without a publicly accessible legal record, L_R cannot be driven to zero without a legal reconstruction effort whose complexity is super-polynomial in |R|. (C3) must hold because without a primary citizen identifier, the GKG's citizen node layer cannot be constructed without O(N²) identity reconciliation work (where N is population size), which is tractable only for very small N. (C4) must hold because without cost-benefit balance within H, the necessary sustained fiscal commitment fails at some point t < H, halting migration.

Sufficiency. Given C1–C4, we show that a migration plan exists that completes within H. By C1, a migration authority exists with power to mandate agency participation. By C2, L_R can be reduced through systematic legal formalization at a rate determinable by the size of the legal corpus and the availability of NLP tools. By C3, the GKG's foundational citizen layer can be constructed in O(N) time through matching against the identifier. By C4, the migration has a valid cost-benefit profile over H. A sequential migration following Theorem 5.2 then completes within H with probability approaching 1 under standard assumptions about institutional persistence. □

