TIGHTENED ABSTRACT (Version 2)

Public administrations worldwide operate on a substrate of paper documents, fragmented databases, dispersed legal rules, and human-mediated workflows. Digital transformation attempts have been architecturally incoherent, producing isolated digital islands rather than sovereign, intelligent state infrastructure. This dissertation introduces the Universal Government Transformation Architecture (UGTA), the first complete formal framework for transforming any public administration into an AI-native, citizen-centric, auditable, and democratically governable organization.

UGTA is grounded in the Administrative Tetrad A = (D, R, W, Dec) — a formally complete, decidable model of government as a computable object — from which a five-layer reference architecture is derived: the Digital Memory Infrastructure, the Government Knowledge Graph, the AI Administrative Agent Network, the Formal Verification and Audit Layer, and the Democratic Governance Shell. Each layer is specified with formal inter-layer contracts, and the critical Formal Verification Layer is elaborated with a complete Provenance Logic, three-check verification algorithm (legal conformance, procedural correctness, normative completeness), and Legal Conformance Certificate schema. A Migration Complexity Theory characterizes transformation distance for any administration and provides an optimal migration sequence with proven optimality. The Cognitive Yield Index offers the first cross-nationally comparable productivity metric for AI-augmented governance, capturing efficiency, quality, and equity simultaneously.

Empirical validation spans five cases: Estonia (paradigm of successful transformation), Singapore (precision deployment), India's India Stack (scale and inclusion), Australia's Robodebt (negative case demonstrating that UGTA would have mechanically prevented the systematic legal errors confirmed by Royal Commission), and Chad (Regime III fragile state strategy). A global implementation roadmap spans 2025–2040, with UGTA positioned as a universal governance standard comparable in architecture and ambition to the Internet Protocol suite.

Keywords: AI-native government, Administrative Tetrad, provenance logic, legal conformance certificate, knowledge graph, algorithmic governance, government transformation, public administration formalization, digital public infrastructure.

